[{:seon.task/name "a-run-boots-one-root-per-test-group"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\"; \"how the fuck do we have a load over 40\". Each shard of a run boots its own shared root per test group (seon.test-store/shared-root!: the default, tuned, merging and pristine roots, about four; the judge-bound one is gone, 2026-11-08), each with a fork server and agents: three sessions proving with four shards each ran 54 ./seon processes at load 40 on 18 cores. bin/test now runs one shard (two with 12 cores free), which divides it by four; this task is the rest: a run's roots are booted once per group for the run (bin/test's plan starts them, the shards ask them by port), so a second shard adds no root, and a group no selected test uses boots none. Measured cost per root: ~3.6 s to listen, ~3 s more to its first agent (the fork server's preload; task seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use). Proofs: a run of two shards over tests of one group boots one root (count the roots a run started; a test of that, written red first, is this task's proof to name); seon.test-store-test/agent-tests-in-one-process-share-its-root still green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/agent-tests-in-one-process-share-its-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-shard-says-what-each-test-waits-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). test side of the sweep (target/waits-test.md in the stability worktree): bin/test waits on its shards in index order (bin/test's wait $P[i]), so a hung shard 1 hides later shards' output; and test waits with no end beyond their answer: ts/ask's socket read of a live parked root, start-root!'s wait for ROOT LISTENING (167), stop-root! (207), kill-root! (178), binary-sh of a mode that parks (113), agent_test's and e2e's <!! event loops on an events tap (agent_test.clj; e2e.clj) that skip a dead agent's event, check.clj's await-event with no terminal event named (204,496,510,517,745,980,1156), bare @p / <!! after a synchronous step in procs_test (282-372), problems_test:176, step_test:11-20 (use the answer helper, procs_test.clj, or realized?). Correct: bin/test reports each shard as it ends (wait -n), and each test wait names its end: an agent's or root's death event ends an event loop (a loop that skips a death is red), a synchronous step's answer is read with realized? (no wait). The parked-everywhere case is jolt.async/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits. Clocks in test (check.clj; lib_test.clj) are listed in target/waits-test.md for docs/clocks.md's owner to name or remove."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/bin-test-reports-shard-ends-index-order-test}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-test-costs-its-boots-not-its-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-06 (docs/testing.md#a-root-costs-what-it-boots): bin/test --all was 1,931 s of tests serial, 9.5 min on 4 shards, 42 tests of 15 s or more being 73 percent of it; they did 2-9 M ticks and waited on root boots. Causes found: in-agent gave its shared root source dirs [\"src\"], so seon.program/required! could not load main's test namespaces through the AOT cache and the fork server evaluated 2,274 forms at its first fork (11.6 s a root, 1.9 s with the config's dirs), and one root per distinct dirs; main-is-proved-at-boot ran all 1,152 tests main held for two verdicts (183 s to 12 s on a store of its own); root-threads-are-what-root-declares ran three process cycles bin/check lifecycle repeats (82 s to 24 s); stores of a test's own imported the checkout and the wiki to prove a store resumes (27 s to 11 s). Done: those four (commits 752525deb9, 39fbccb1fb). Open: one shared root per distinct :sets in agent-test (a tuned root for child-min-ms, jobs, node-cache-bytes, heap-extra-bytes serves them all: 7 s a variant a shard); own-root agent tests whose subject is not the root's life can use the shared root; the root's boot (5 s, about 3 s of it not yet attributed) and the fork server's first fork (2.7 s) are the floor of every process test."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/agent-tests-sets-differ-only-tuned-keys-share}}
 {:seon.task/name "three-tests-seed-more-than-their-claim-needs"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-07, by which verdicts hold seon.db/seed!: 33 tests seed on every run, 1.35 G ticks of a 28.6 G pass (4.7 percent); the rest of the seeds are a shard's first test taking the process's one in-memory store, which a clone cannot help. Most of the 33 test store creation itself, so no shared seeded store is worth building. Three are worth shrinking to their claims, about 2 percent: seon.proofs-test/a-process-plans-on-more-scratch-stores-than-its-undo-entries (235 M ticks, 12 seeds), seon.check-test/a-check-leaves-its-store-as-it-found-it (205 M, up from 36 M on 2026-11-06) and seon.prove-test/a-commit-starts-no-run-of-main-unless-the-config-says-so (141 M). Proof: each still red on the mutation it guards, at a smaller seed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/proofs-test-process-plans-more-scratch-stores-undo}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-system-test-ends-at-its-roots-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Tests. Found 2026-11-12 10:13 (jolt/tests' prove): with seon.reconcile/done? deleted, the test roots' server answered every request that summarizes tasks (seon.server/live-tasks -> task-summaries -> r/done?) with :seon.server/defect \"No such var: r/done?\" AT ONCE (bin/ask got it in under a second), yet about 30 seon.agent-test system tests each ran to their 300 s bound, parked on their socket to the root (bin/stalls' sample: kevent; lsof: one TCP connection to the root). So the root answered, and something on the test's side, or inside the tested agent's work (its turn reads live tasks too), treated the error as not yet and waited again. What allowed it: a wait whose only end is the bound when the thing it waits on answers an error (AGENTS.md section 4: every wait ends, with its value or a typed error). Plan: reproduce at n=1 (one with-agent test on a root whose server throws on task summaries), read which wait parked (a fiber dump of the test process and of the root at second 10), and make that wait end on the error it was given, at its cause; sweep test/seon for the same shape (a loop over root answers or events that recurs on anything but the one success). Proof, red first: that test ends within seconds with the root's error named, where today it runs to its bound."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-a-process-makes-is-its-agents-and-goes-at-its-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Needs runtime's fork-one (schedule item: a test run is an agent forked from its parent agent). Today seon.test-store/reclaim! sweeps after the fact: what a test process makes outside itself (a shared root's merges branch, its store main and runs dir, a fixture worktree) is named with its pid (mark-of), and the next shard removes what a gone pid made (kill -0); root, 2026-11-12: a stopgap. Plan: once fork-one lands, what a process makes is a fact on its agent entity (each branch, worktree and run dir it made, written as it makes it), and the system removes them at that agent's exit event, the same event that ends its run. Deletes: mark-of, reclaim!, dead-pid?, start-shared-root!'s shutdown hook, the fixtures' finally removals. Proof, red first: an agent that made a branch and a worktree and was killed leaves neither once its exit is recorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-root-ends-with-the-run-that-started-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests; the structural fix is runtime's fork-one (family C of design/one-unit-spec.md: a test root becomes a child agent, stopped with its parent). Found 2026-11-12 (data): stopping a prove at 14:37 left a test's detached root (bin/held --detach, ./seon root --profile scripted) orphaned for 52 min, and bin/land later named its pids as processes working in data's worktree. A detached root is double-forked out of its starter's session on purpose, so its starter's end does not reach it. Plan: once fork-one lands, a system-test's root is the run's child agent and ends at the run's stop; until then, the test that starts a root records it on its run (mark-of's name, as reclaim! reads), and reclaim! ends a root whose maker is gone. Proof, red first: a run stopped while its test's root runs leaves no process of that root once the next shard starts."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
